github.com/digitorus/pdf v0.1.2 — modified by nib
==================================================

This directory is github.com/digitorus/pdf at v0.1.2, copied unchanged from the Go module cache
except for TWO files, read.go and lex.go, and this notice. go.mod replaces the module with this
directory (`replace github.com/digitorus/pdf => ./third_party/digitorus-pdf`); the require line still
pins v0.1.2. The upstream licence is LICENSE, unchanged (BSD-style, The Go Authors).

Why: /pending 758, ADR-066 (which supersedes ADR-041's refusal of this option in part); lex.go:
/pending 761, ADR-068 (which widens ADR-066's read.go-only scope to this one lex.go change).

What changed, in read.go only
-----------------------------

`(*Reader).resolve` found an object-stream member by decoding the stream FROM ITS START and lexing
its `id offset` header pairs until the id matched, then seeking forward to the member — for every
member, with nothing cached. pdfsign's verify and nib's revision sweep each resolve every xref entry,
so a stream of N members cost N²/2 header pairs and N²/2 decoded bytes per pass. An honest InDesign
file (2.7 MB) paid 3.2 s a pass for it.

The patched resolve keeps, per Reader, one `objStmCache` per object stream:

  - the decoder, kept open, and the bytes decoded SO FAR — extended lazily, only as far as a read
    needs, so a corrupt zlib tail or a short /Length after the wanted member is never reached,
    exactly as upstream never reached it;
  - the header pairs lexed SO FAR — lexed lazily up to the wanted id, first listing wins, ids
    truncated to uint32 as upstream compared them; a token that is not an integer reads as 0, and
    once the header reaches its end every later pair reads as (0, 0), as upstream's loop read them;
  - a panic raised part-way (by the lexer or the decoder), raised again by any later lookup that
    needs to go past that point.

Each lookup reads its member from the member's own offset, over the cached bytes. The checks on the
stream's dictionary (/Type, /N, /First, /Extends) are still made on every lookup, from a fresh read
of it, and the /Extends walk is unchanged.

The cache key is every input the decoded bytes depend on: the stream's own objptr (which decryption
keys on), its data offset, and its raw /Length, /Filter and /DecodeParms.

No mutex: every Reader is used by one call on one goroutine (nib's internal/sign and pdfsign's
sign and verify each open a Reader, use it and drop it).

The declared divergences
------------------------

1. Upstream read the member by seeking the HEADER'S OWN buffer. A /First pointing behind that buffer's
current 4 KB chunk is a backward seek, which set a negative buffer index and panicked. The patched
reader reads the member from its offset and resolves it. No real producer writes such a /First (the
header precedes the members); a member at a NEGATIVE offset still panics.

2. (/pending 760) The patched reader refuses, with a panic carrying ErrObjStmTooCostly, (a) member
reads of one stream that together consume more than four times what the stream has decoded (plus 4 KB
a stream and 64 bytes a lookup), and (b) decoding more than 64 MiB of one stream. Honest members do not
overlap, so their reads together consume about what the stream decodes to; the shapes this refuses —
many header ids naming one offset, offsets overlapping into one nested member, one member running past
64 MiB — upstream would read, at 44 s a pass for 400 ids at one 1 MB member and 30 s for 8,000 offsets
into one nested array. nib's cost model cannot see a member's extent without paying for it.

3. (/pending 761) The patched reader refuses, with a panic carrying ErrObjStmRunsPastEnd, a read of an
object stream that is told the stream has ended more than 64 times. Past the end upstream's readByte
answers '\n' for ever, so an array, hex string or literal string left open at the stream's clean end
never terminates: the hex string spins, the other two allocate until the process runs out of memory.
pdfcpu reads all three documents, so nib's gate in front of this reader does not keep them out. An
honest read is told of the end a few times (its last token's delimiter, one look-ahead). The fuzz test
below cannot compare this class — the original never returns — so it is held by internal/sign's
TestAMemberLeftOpenAtTheStreamsEndIsRefusedNotRead instead.

4. (/pending 761, the only change in lex.go) `(*buffer).readArray` refuses an `endobj` or `endstream`
token, panicking "unexpected keyword %q parsing array" through the lexer's own errorf. Upstream's
readObject unreads `endobj` and answers null (so `5 0 obj endobj` is a null object, which is
unchanged), and readArray, seeing a token that is neither `]` nor nil, handed it back to readObject
and appended that null for ever — anywhere, not only in an object stream, until the process ran out of
memory. pdfcpu reads such a document, so nib's gate does not keep it out. `endstream` already panicked
inside readObject; it is named so the loop's exits say what an array may not contain. readDict needs
no change: a keyword where a key belongs already panics ("unexpected non-name key"). The same test
holds this class, for the same reason.

internal/sign's FuzzThePatchedObjectStreamReaderMatchesTheOriginal (/pending 759) resolves random object
streams through this reader and through v0.1.2 itself (a test-only copy under internal/sign/testdata)
and allows exactly the first two classes of difference; it never generates the third or fourth, where
the original never returns.

Maintenance
-----------

Re-apply this patch at every bump of github.com/digitorus/pdf. The guard in nib's root package
(`TestTheLibraryPatchIsReadGoAndLexGoOnlyAsItsNoticeSays`) fails when go.mod's require moves off
v0.1.2, when the replace stops pointing here, when either change is reverted, or when any file other
than read.go and lex.go differs from the module-cache original.

Reporting upstream is Dan's call.
